  "Number": 8,
  "Question": "Given that \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) are distinct nonzero real numbers such that \\(x + \\dfrac{2}{x} = y + \\dfrac{2}{y},\\) what is \\(xy?\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\)",
    "C": "\\(1\\)",
    "D": "\\(2\\)",
    "E": "\\(4\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "Clearing the fractions will reveal a common factor. Because \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) are nonzero, multiplying the equation by \\(xy\\) is reversible and gives\n\\[\nx^2y+2y=xy^2+2x.\n\\]\nMove the terms involving \\(xy\\) to one side and factor:\n\\[\nx^2y-xy^2=2x-2y,\n\\qquad\nxy(x-y)=2(x-y).\n\\]\nThe numbers are distinct, so \\(x-y\\ne0\\). Dividing by this common factor gives \\(xy=2\\). All canceled quantities are nonzero under the stated conditions, so the answer is \\(2\\), choice D.",
